# Module and notebook slash-command responses

When finishing `/write-module-readme`, `/new-lesson`, `/write-lesson`,
`/validate-notebook`, or `/review-module`, keep the **user-facing reply
minimal**.

## Do not include

- Pass / OK / "Verdict: Pass" sections or checklists with all-green rows
- Tables summarizing what already meets standards
- Repeated standards text from docs
- Full notebook or module recaps unless the author asked for them

## `/write-module-readme`

- Path of the module README created
- One line: next steps → review the README, explicitly set the module to
  `Started`, then run `/new-lesson`
- Only mention blocking design gaps if creation could not be completed

## `/new-lesson`

- Path of the scaffold created
- One line: next step → `/write-lesson`, then `/validate-notebook`
- Only mention gaps if scaffolding could not be completed

## `/write-lesson`

- Path edited
- One line: next step → `/validate-notebook`, then Databricks runtime validation
- Only mention blockers (missing scaffold, missing Focus cell topic, etc.)

## `/validate-notebook`

**Issues only.**

- If nothing fails: one line — e.g. `No authoring issues found.` — then note
  the author should run the notebook in Azure Databricks for runtime validation
- If something fails: bullet list — `[file]` or `[file ~lines]` → what's wrong → specific fix
- Group by topic only when there are many issues; never list passed checks
- Mark non-blocking polish as `(optional)`; do not mix with blocking issues
- Do not restate review checklist categories as Pass/Fail tables

## `/review-module`

**Issues only.**

- If nothing fails: one line — e.g. `No authoring issues found.`
- If something fails: bullet list — `[file]` or `[file ~lines]` → what's wrong → specific fix
- Group by topic only when there are many issues; never list passed checks
- Mark non-blocking polish as `(optional)`; do not mix with blocking issues
- Do not restate review checklist categories as Pass/Fail tables
